course: dictionary
lesson: 8266
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one you need. Ridiculous means very silly or unreasonable, so people may laugh at it. «Take those ridiculous things off!» Here the Rat wants the Mole's silly motor-clothes gone.
explain: Ridiculous also has an informal meaning: extreme or amazing. «The price was ridiculous.» Nobody is laughing at a price; it is much too high. Ask which meaning fits the sentence, then put your meaning in place of the word to check.
explain: Writers use ridiculous to show what they think. «I call it simply ridiculous.» tells you the speaker thinks something is silly and not sensible. Ridiculous is an adjective, and its adverb is ridiculously.
fact: Ridiculous means very silly or unreasonable, so that people may laugh at it.
fact: In informal talk, ridiculous can mean extreme or amazing, as in «The price was ridiculous.»
fact: The forms are ridiculous, more ridiculous and most ridiculous. The adverb is ridiculously.
fact: In Alice in Wonderland, almost everything is ridiculous, such as a Caucus-race where everyone wins.
fact: In maths, a ridiculous answer, like a child 50 metres tall, means you should check your working and ask «does this answer make sense?»
exercise: «Take those ridiculous things off!» What does ridiculous mean here? | Extremely expensive | Very quiet | *Very silly, so people may laugh | Made of wood
exercise: «The price was ridiculous.» What does ridiculous mean here? | Clean and shiny | *Extreme, far too high | Kind and friendly | Very silly, so people laugh
exercise: In maths you get the answer that a child is 50 metres tall. What should you do? | Change the question | Cheer, because you are clever | Write it down and move on | *Check your working and ask if the answer makes sense
test: What is the adverb form of ridiculous? | ridicule | ridiculing | ridiculousness | *ridiculously
test: «I call it simply ridiculous.» What does the speaker think? | That it is very old | That it is very quiet | That it is a good price | *That it is silly and not sensible
exam: Which sentence uses ridiculous to mean extreme? | «The path was ridiculous.» | «The dog was ridiculous.» | «The hat was ridiculous.» | *«The price was ridiculous.»
